##### Data Set of Southern Oscillations {#sec-pmc-4412}

[**Source and Background**]{#sec-pmc-4412-background}

The southern oscillation is defined as the barametric pressure difference between Tahiti and the Darwin Islands at sea level. The southern oscillation is a predictor of el nino which in turn is thought to be a driver of world-wide weather. Specifically, repeated southern oscillation values less than -1 typically defines an el nino. Note: the decimal values in the second column of the data given below are obtained as (month number - 0.5)/12. The reader can download the data as a [text file](../../datasets/ELNINO.DAT).

