The partial autocorrelation plot suggests that an AR(2) model might be appropriate since the partial autocorrelation becomes zero after the second lag. The lag 12 is also significant, indicating some remaining seasonality.

In summary, our initial attempt would be to fit an AR(2) model with a seasonal AR(12) term on the data with a linear trend line removed. We could try the model both with and without seasonal differencing applied. Model validation should be performed before accepting this as a final model.


##### Partial Autocorrelation Plot {#sec-pmc-4463}

[**Purpose: Model Identification for Box-Jenkins Models**]{#sec-pmc-4463-purpose}

Partial autocorrelation plots (Box and Jenkins, pp. 64-65 [-@BoxJenkinsReinsel1994]) are a commonly used tool for model identification in Box-Jenkins models.

The partial autocorrelation at lag $k$ is the autocorrelation between $X_t$ and $X_{t-k}$ that is not accounted for by lags 1 through $k-1$.

There are algorithms, not discussed here, for computing the partial autocorrelation based on the sample autocorrelations. See [@BoxJenkinsReinsel1994] or [@Brockwell1987] for the mathematical details.

::: {.callout-note}
NIST's original page cites this material as "Box, Jenkins, and Reinsel 1970" and "Brockwell, 1991", but links both citations to the same reference-list entries used throughout this chapter: Box, Jenkins, and Reinsel's 3rd edition (1994) and Brockwell and Davis's *Time Series: Theory and Methods* (1987) — Chapter 6's reference list has no 1970 or 1991 editions. We cite the 1994 and 1987 editions here to match the linked entries.
:::

Specifically, partial autocorrelations are useful in identifying the order of an [autoregressive model](#sec-pmc-444-ar). The partial autocorrelation of an AR($p$) process is zero at lag $p+1$ and greater. If the sample autocorrelation plot indicates that an AR model may be appropriate, then the sample partial autocorrelation plot is examined to help identify the order. We look for the point on the plot where the partial autocorrelations essentially become zero. Placing a 95% confidence interval for statistical significance is helpful for this purpose.

The approximate 95% confidence interval for the partial autocorrelations are at $\pm 2/\sqrt{N}$.

[**Sample Plot**]{#sec-pmc-4463-sample-plot}


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true"}
library(tidyverse)

elnino_data <- read_table(
  here::here("datasets", "ELNINO.DAT"),
  skip = 25,
  col_names = c("SouthernOscillation", "YearMonth", "Year", "Month")
) |>
  drop_na()

pacf_plot_bj(elnino_data$SouthernOscillation, lag_max = 114, title = "Southern Oscillations")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![Partial autocorrelation plot of southern oscillations data](index_files/figure-html/fig-pmc-4463-sample-plot-1.png){#fig-pmc-4463-sample-plot width=672}
:::
:::


This partial autocorrelation plot, for the [southern oscillations](#sec-pmc-4412) data set, shows clear statistical significance for lags 1 and 2 (lag 0 is always 1). The next few lags are at the borderline of statistical significance. If the autocorrelation plot indicates that an AR model is appropriate, we could start our modeling with an AR(2) model. We might compare this with an AR(3) model.

[**Definition**]{#sec-pmc-4463-definition}

Partial autocorrelation plots are formed by

| | |
|---|---|
| Vertical axis: | Partial autocorrelation coefficient at lag $h$. |
| Horizontal axis: | Time lag $h \, (h=0, \, 1, \, 2, \, 3, \, \ldots)$. |

In addition, 95% confidence interval bands are typically included on the plot.

[**Questions**]{#sec-pmc-4463-questions}

The partial autocorrelation plot can help provide answers to the following questions:

1. Is an AR model appropriate for the data?
2. If an AR model is appropriate, what order should we use?

[**Related Techniques**]{#sec-pmc-4463-related}

- Autocorrelation Plot
- Run Sequence Plot
- Spectral Plot

[**Case Study**]{#sec-pmc-4463-case-study}

The partial autocorrelation plot is demonstrated in the Negiz data case study.

[**Software**]{#sec-pmc-4463-software}

Partial autocorrelation plots are available in many general purpose statistical software programs.


#### Box-Jenkins Model Estimation {#sec-pmc-447}

[**Use Software**]{#sec-pmc-447-use-software}

Estimating the parameters for the Box-Jenkins models is a quite complicated non-linear estimation problem. For this reason, the parameter estimation should be left to a high quality software program that fits Box-Jenkins models. Fortunately, many commercial statistical software programs now fit Box-Jenkins models.

[**Approaches**]{#sec-pmc-447-approaches}

The main approaches to fitting Box-Jenkins models are non-linear least squares and maximum likelihood estimation.

Maximum likelihood estimation is generally the preferred technique. The likelihood equations for the full Box-Jenkins model are complicated and are not included here. See Brockwell and Davis [-@Brockwell1987] for the mathematical details.

[**Model Estimation Example**]{#sec-pmc-447-example}

The Negiz case study shows an example of the Box-Jenkins model-fitting.


#### Box-Jenkins Model Diagnostics {#sec-pmc-448}

[**Assumptions for a Stable Univariate Process**]{#sec-pmc-448-assumptions}

Model diagnostics for Box-Jenkins models is similar to model validation for non-linear least squares fitting.

That is, the error term $A_t$ is assumed to follow the assumptions for a stationary univariate process. The residuals should be white noise (or independent when their distributions are normal) drawings from a fixed distribution with a constant mean and variance. If the Box-Jenkins model is a good model for the data, the residuals should satisfy these assumptions.

If these assumptions are not satisfied, we need to fit a more appropriate model. That is, we go back to the model identification step and try to develop a better model. Hopefully the analysis of the residuals can provide some clues as to a more appropriate model.

[**4-Plot of Residuals**]{#sec-pmc-448-4-plot}

As discussed in the EDA chapter, one way to assess if the residuals from the Box-Jenkins model follow the assumptions is to generate a 4-plot of the residuals and an autocorrelation plot of the residuals. One could also look at the value of the Box-Ljung [-@Ljung1978] statistic.

An example of analyzing the residuals from a Box-Jenkins model is given in the Negiz data case study.


##### Box-Ljung Test {#sec-pmc-4481}

[**Test for Lack of Fit**]{#sec-pmc-4481-test}

The Box-Ljung test [-@Ljung1978] is a diagnostic tool used to test the lack of fit of a time series model.

The test is applied to the residuals of a time series after fitting an ARMA($p,q$) model to the data. The test examines $m$ autocorrelations of the residuals. If the autocorrelations are very small, we conclude that the model does not exhibit significant lack of fit.

Section [6.4.4.9](#sec-pmc-449) contains an example in which the Box-Ljung test is implemented using a residual time series.

[**Definition**]{#sec-pmc-4481-definition}

In general, the Box-Ljung test is defined as:

| | |
|---|---|
| $H_0$: | The model does not exhibit lack of fit. |
| $H_a$: | The model exhibits lack of fit. |
| Test Statistic: | Given a time series $Y$ of length $n$, the test statistic is defined as: $$ Q = n(n+2)\sum_{k=1}^{m} \, \frac{\hat{r}^2_k}{n-k} $$ where $\hat{r}_k$ is the estimated autocorrelation of the series at lag $k$, and $m$ is the number of lags being tested. |
| Significance Level: | $\alpha$ |
| Critical Region: | The Box-Ljung test rejects the null hypothesis (indicating that the model has significant lack of fit) if $$ Q > \chi_{1-\alpha, \, h}^2 $$ where $\chi_{1-\alpha, \, h}^2$ is the chi-square distribution table value with $h$ degrees of freedom and significance level $\alpha$. Because the test is applied to residuals, the degrees of freedom must account for the estimated model parameters so that $h = m-p-q$, where $p$ and $q$ indicate the number of parameters from the ARMA($p,q$) model fit to the data. |


